00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.083
GitLab design management allows you to upload

00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:43.583
images like wireframes or mockups to a GitLab issue.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.791
The goal is to store them in one place, but for us

00:01:46.791 --> 00:01:50.541
it's enough to know that it allows attaching images to issues.

00:01:51.375 --> 00:01:54.166
And text in issues supports markdown.

00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:58.250
It's a very light markup language for creating formatted text.

00:01:58.708 --> 00:02:00.833
Among others, it supports links.

00:02:01.208 --> 00:02:06.791
This is the syntax you use, which is then transformed to this HTML code.

00:02:06.791 --> 00:02:11.708
And the links that were leading to an image from a design
 were treated differently.

00:02:12.125 --> 00:02:16.375
The issue ID and the filename were shown as the link's content.

00:02:17.083 --> 00:02:21.125
So GitLab needed to know if your link leads to a design or not.

00:02:21.458 --> 00:02:24.083
For that, regular expressions were used.

00:02:25.041 --> 00:02:30.985
First, the server checked
 if the path was linking to a design using path_segment regex.

00:02:30.985 --> 00:02:33.458
Nothing out of line was noticed here.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:38.500
But then it extracted the filename using this regex.

00:02:39.083 --> 00:02:43.500
It means any character that is not a forward slash or whitespace,

00:02:43.666 --> 00:02:46.958
followed by a dot and followed by an extension.

00:02:47.791 --> 00:02:51.958
It means that a quote before the dot is perfectly fine.

00:02:52.541 --> 00:02:55.083
And your link would eventually be present

00:02:55.083 --> 00:02:58.250
in the href attribute of an a tag.

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.791
So you could use this double quote to escape this attribute.

00:03:02.833 --> 00:03:08.208
But for that to actually work,
 you would have to upload a file with such filename first.

00:03:08.875 --> 00:03:11.208
It was a problem because the sanitizer

00:03:11.208 --> 00:03:13.958
would not allow double quotes in the filename.

00:03:14.791 --> 00:03:18.625
However, when using this strange content disposition header,

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.375
you bypassed the workhorse and bypassed the sanitizer.

00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:25.875
This unusual syntax means

00:03:26.021 --> 00:03:29.708
that the filename is written using ASCII-8bit charset.

00:03:29.708 --> 00:03:35.875
The empty value between quotes is a place for a language
 which is apparently not required.

00:03:36.375 --> 00:03:41.083
Then,
 there is the filename, which in this case is just a standard string.

00:03:42.062 --> 00:03:44.625
What probably happens, is the sanitizer

00:03:44.708 --> 00:03:49.500
can't parse this filename or does not support this charset
 so it just ignores it,

00:03:49.541 --> 00:03:53.291
thus allowing files with quotes in their names.

00:03:54.666 --> 00:03:56.222
How do you then execute the 

00:03:56.222 --> 00:03:59.708
XSS if you can't use spaces or forward slashes?

00:04:00.125 --> 00:04:00.882
You could use 

00:04:00.882 --> 00:04:06.583
a new tag without any attributes
 but you would be blocked by content security policy.

00:04:06.992 --> 00:04:10.625
But turns out that when you have multiple attributes,

00:04:10.791 --> 00:04:14.041
you don't need a space or a slash to separate them.

00:04:14.458 --> 00:04:18.541
If you use quotes, you can use one attribute next to another.

00:04:20.083 --> 00:04:25.208
This way, you can inject arbitrary attributes to this a tag.

00:04:25.208 --> 00:04:29.541
How to now turn it into an XSS?

00:04:29.541 --> 00:04:32.958
Injecting any event handlers will not work because it would

00:04:32.958 --> 00:04:35.625
also be blocked by content security policy.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:40.666
But turns out that ReferenceRedactor has a functionality

00:04:40.666 --> 00:04:43.291
that might be just the right fit for the job.

00:04:44.458 --> 00:04:47.541
It takes the value of data-original attribute

00:04:47.541 --> 00:04:51.208
and puts it inside the innerHTML of a link.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:55.000
So you create a design with such payload.

00:04:55.375 --> 00:04:59.041
It closes the original quote that will be after the link,

00:04:59.666 --> 00:05:04.791
then it adds another attribute class="gfm" which just makes sure

00:05:04.833 --> 00:05:08.166
that we will go through desired, vulnerable functions.

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:11.291
Next, it adds yet another attribute.

00:05:11.416 --> 00:05:15.166
This time it's unclosed with only one single quote.

00:05:16.666 --> 00:05:19.125
Thanks to leaving this attribute unclosed,

00:05:19.208 --> 00:05:23.208
we will be able to put our payload in the issue's comment

00:05:23.208 --> 00:05:27.250
instead of putting the whole payload in the filename.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:30.916
Then, you add this as the issue's comment,

00:05:31.333 --> 00:05:33.958
referencing your file with malicious name.

00:05:35.083 --> 00:05:38.208
Markdown does allow you to use HTML,

00:05:38.208 --> 00:05:41.333
but of course, easy XSS will not work here.

00:05:42.791 --> 00:05:45.375
As the content of the link, you add this,

00:05:45.666 --> 00:05:48.375
with one single quote at the beginning

00:05:48.375 --> 00:05:51.833
and then arbitrary attributes that you want to inject.

00:05:51.833 --> 00:05:56.000
As you are no longer in the filename, you can now use spaces.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.000
So you use the

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.875
data-original attribute with arbitrary HTML code.

00:06:01.875 --> 00:06:05.291
We'll come back in a second to this specific payload.

00:06:05.875 --> 00:06:10.625
You also need to add a few more attributes in order to avoid exceptions.

00:06:11.666 --> 00:06:14.958
Then, the first transformation puts your content

00:06:14.958 --> 00:06:19.625
as an actual attribute of the a tag and then ReferenceRedactor

00:06:20.458 --> 00:06:22.750
takes the value of this data-original

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:27.666
and puts the contents into an actual content of the tag.

00:06:27.666 --> 00:06:31.989
This way, you can inject arbitrary HTML to GitLab issue.

00:06:32.667 --> 00:06:36.166
But the next problem is content security policy.

00:06:36.583 --> 00:06:39.250
It blocks executing an inline script

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:42.958
so you can't use a script tag with any JavaScript code.

00:06:44.125 --> 00:06:47.125
However, CSP allows content from

00:06:47.218 --> 00:06:53.750
apis.google.com domain and this one serves JSONP endpoint.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:58.250
It works the way that in the URL, you tell Google server

00:06:58.333 --> 00:07:01.958
what contents you want to be present in the JavaScript file.

00:07:02.625 --> 00:07:05.708
As the callback parameter, you give the function name

00:07:05.708 --> 00:07:09.083
and as the q parameter, you give the first argument.

00:07:09.791 --> 00:07:12.375
So when calling it with this URL,

00:07:12.375 --> 00:07:16.208
it will call setTimeout function and execute the alert.

00:07:17.625 --> 00:07:20.208
So that's the full cross-site scripting.

00:07:20.458 --> 00:07:25.250
It's crazy how many things had to align for this to be possible.

00:07:25.875 --> 00:07:30.375
Regular expression for the file name, then bypassing the validator,

00:07:30.791 --> 00:07:35.958
using data-original attribute and finally, bypassing the CSP.

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:40.541
But the best thing is that it's not even the end

00:07:40.541 --> 00:07:44.333
because William escalated this XSS even further

00:07:45.125 --> 00:07:49.541
because five days later, he submitted another comment in this report.

00:07:51.125 --> 00:07:56.041
In GitLab,
 there is a functionality to send e-mail notifications about issues.

00:07:56.208 --> 00:07:59.500
And their content is present in such messages.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:06.791
And premailer-rails will try to fetch all the external CSS files
 and put them inside this email

00:08:07.083 --> 00:08:10.833
so that the mail doesn't require external content.

00:08:11.583 --> 00:08:15.000
So for each stylesheet, it will take the href attribute

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.083
and try to fetch it on the server before sending an email.

00:08:19.833 --> 00:08:24.916
It can be an HTTP link, but it can also lead to a local file.

00:08:25.666 --> 00:08:30.750
In the first comment,
 William presented a way to leak a last line of any file,

00:08:30.916 --> 00:08:35.250
but in another one he showed a way to leak whole contents.

00:08:36.375 --> 00:08:38.166
If you use such tag, 

00:08:38.166 --> 00:08:41.166
the server will try to embed contents of

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:44.000
/etc/passwd file in the CSS. 

00:08:44.875 --> 00:08:49.375
However, this one alone would break the CSS syntax.

00:08:49.375 --> 00:08:52.875
In order to avoid it, fetch the opening

00:08:52.875 --> 00:08:56.083
and closing of this expression from your own server.

00:08:57.166 --> 00:09:00.958
The content in between will be the leaked part from the server,

00:09:00.958 --> 00:09:03.333
and it will not cause any exceptions.

00:09:04.541 --> 00:09:07.208
This way, you can read any file present

00:09:07.208 --> 00:09:09.791
on the server, including files like

00:09:09.791 --> 00:09:13.083
secrets.yaml or gitlab.yaml. 

00:09:13.083 --> 00:09:14.843
You can also execute an 

00:09:14.843 --> 00:09:17.250
SSRF attack this way. 

00:09:17.583 --> 00:09:20.958
The report has been awarded $16,000,

00:09:20.958 --> 00:09:24.500
which is the middle of the range for critical bug in GitLab.

